  "fresh_frog": {
    "title": "THIS FROG CAN BREATHE THROUGH ITS SKIN",
    "script": "A frog does not rely only on its lungs. Its thin, moist skin can exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide directly with the environment, which is why frogs must avoid drying out. Many also absorb water through a specialized patch underneath the body instead of drinking through the mouth. Their eyes can help with swallowing by pressing downward as food moves toward the throat. During cold weather, some frogs rest underwater or in mud and continue taking in oxygen through the skin. This animal is not simply wet. Its entire surface is part lung, part water bottle, and part chemical shield.",
