  "fresh_owl": {
    "title": "OWLS ARE BUILT TO HEAR IN THREE DIMENSIONS",
    "script": "An owl's face is more than a face. The ring of feathers around it works like a sound collector, guiding tiny noises toward the ears. In many species, one ear opening sits higher than the other, so the brain can compare when a sound reaches each side and locate prey in three dimensions. Large forward-facing eyes gather light, but they cannot roll inside their sockets. That is why the neck does so much work. Some owls can rotate the head about 270 degrees, while specialized blood vessels keep the brain supplied. Then soft fringed feathers reduce flight noise before the strike.",
