  "greater_flamingo": {
    "title": "FLAMINGOS ARE PINK BECAUSE OF THEIR FOOD",
    "script": "A flamingo is not born bright pink. Pigments called carotenoids enter its body through algae and small crustaceans, then color the feathers as they grow. Its strange bill works upside down. The bird lowers its head, pumps water with the tongue, and traps food against comb-like plates called lamellae. Long legs reach feeding areas that shorter birds cannot use, while webbed feet stir the bottom. The famous one-legged pose may also reduce heat loss by keeping one leg tucked into warm feathers. So the color, bill, legs, and posture all connect to one demanding life in shallow, salty wetlands.",
