  "meerkat_sentinels": {
    "title": "MEERKATS RUN A LIVING SECURITY SYSTEM",
    "script": "In the open deserts of southern Africa, a meerkat group cannot let everyone search for food at the same time. While others dig for insects and small animals, one individual often climbs to a high point and becomes a sentinel. It scans the sky and ground, then gives quiet calls that tell the group the watch is active. If danger appears, the alarm changes with urgency and can trigger an immediate dash for cover. Research shows that some individuals guard more often than others, creating so-called super sentinels. Young meerkats also learn dangerous prey handling gradually. Adults may disable a scorpion before offering it to a pup, then provide more challenging prey as the youngster improves. Dark patches around the eyes help reduce glare while watching the horizon, and long claws turn hard soil into escape tunnels. The result is not one heroic lookout but a cooperative network: food, warning, teaching, and shelter all depend on the group. A single meerkat is alert. A mob of them becomes an organized survival system.",
