  "humpback_whale": {
    "title": "HUMPBACK WHALES BUILD NETS FROM BUBBLES",
    "script": "A humpback whale can hunt with a net made from nothing but air. In cold feeding grounds, one whale may dive beneath a school of fish and release bubbles while swimming in a circle. The rising curtain crowds the prey into a tighter column. Sometimes several whales coordinate their positions and calls, then surge upward together with their mouths open, swallowing fish and seawater before baleen plates strain the meal. This technique is called bubble-net feeding, and different populations use different patterns, suggesting that parts of the behavior spread through social learning. Humpbacks also use their exceptionally long pectoral fins to maneuver and sometimes help corral prey. After the feeding season, many travel thousands of miles toward warmer breeding waters. Adult males produce long, structured songs that change over time and can be repeated for hours. The same animal that erupts from the surface in a breach can also communicate across dark water and cooperate around an invisible trap. The final weapon is not strength alone. It is shared timing: bubbles, sound, and multiple whales turning scattered fish into one concentrated target.",
