  "cheetah_biomechanics": {
    "title": "A CHEETAH TURNS ITS ENTIRE BODY INTO A SPRING",
    "script": "A cheetah does not win by maintaining top speed for long. It wins through a short mechanical explosion. The spine flexes and extends like a spring, allowing the front and rear legs to spread far apart and lengthen every stride. Semi-retractable claws stay exposed enough to grip the ground like cleats, while specialized paw pads add traction. Large nasal passages, lungs, and a powerful heart move oxygen quickly during the chase. But speed without steering would be useless. The long muscular tail swings like a counterweight, helping the cat change direction while following prey that can cut sharply across the grass. High-set eyes keep the target in view, and dark facial marks may reduce glare. The body pays a cost for this design: the sprint creates enormous heat and cannot last very long. After a kill, the cheetah must recover while larger predators may steal the meal. It also cannot roar like a lion; cheetahs chirp, purr, hiss, and call to one another. The fastest land mammal is therefore not simply a powerful cat. It is a lightweight, flexible racing system built for acceleration, turning, and one brief chance to succeed.",
