  "giraffe_pressure": {
    "title": "A GIRAFFE'S HEART FIGHTS GRAVITY EVERY SECOND",
    "script": "A giraffe's head may rise several meters above its heart, so sending blood to the brain requires exceptional pressure. The heart has a thick muscular wall, and the circulatory system maintains blood pressure far above typical human levels. But drinking creates the opposite problem. When the giraffe spreads its front legs and lowers the head, gravity could drive too much blood toward the brain. Valves, elastic vessels, and pressure-regulating structures help control that sudden shift. Tight skin and connective tissue around the lower legs also act like natural compression garments, limiting fluid buildup. The neck itself still contains only seven cervical vertebrae, the same basic number found in most mammals, but each bone is dramatically elongated. Even the feeding equipment is specialized: a long, prehensile tongue wraps around branches and helps pull leaves from thorny trees. Then the animal has to raise its head again without fainting. A giraffe looks calm because the engineering is hidden. Every drink, every lifted head, and every step depends on a cardiovascular system continuously correcting for height and gravity.",
